==Trivia==
*In the {{WPS|Blade (franchise)|''Blade''}} franchise, Eric Brooks is a [[human]]-[[vampire]] [[hybrid]] whose mother was bitten by {{WPS|Deacon Frost}} while giving birth to Brooks, causing not only his mother's death, but Brooks to become a hybrid who has no weaknesses of vampires besides bloodlust, which he uses a serum for. Brooks eventually pursued Frost and found that Brooks' mother was alive the whole time being kept as a vampire by Frost. This forced Blade to not only kill Frost, but his own mother. Blade briefly joined a group called the Bloodpack as well as the Nightstalkers, which helped him fight Jared Nomak and {{WPS|Dracula (Marvel Comics)|Dracula}} respectively. Blade hesitantly began to work with [[w:c:marvel:Krista Starr|Krista Starr]] to defeat [[w:c:marvel:Marcus Van Sciver|Marcus Van Sciver]], [[w:c:marvel:Charlotte (Earth-26320)|Charlotte]], and the rest of the [[w:c:marvel:House of Chthon|House of Chthon]].
*Blade is one character who was portrayed by an actor that exists in the Marvel Cinematic Universe without portraying themselves, alongside [[Mainframe]] and [[Gambit]]. [[Wesley Snipes]]' existence in the MCU was confirmed in the ''[[Luke Cage (TV series)|Luke Cage]]'' episode ''[[Now You're Mine]]''.
*Following his appearance as Blade in ''[[Deadpool & Wolverine]]'', [[Wesley Snipes]] broke two {{WPS|Guinness World Records}}: the longest career as a live-action Marvel character, surpassing [[Hugh Jackman]], and the longest gap between character appearances in Marvel films.<ref>[https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2024/7/wesley-snipes-steals-hugh-jackmans-record-with-shock-deadpool-wolverine-cameo Wesley Snipes steals Hugh Jackman's record with shock Deadpool & Wolverine cameo]</ref>

==Behind the Scenes==
*Blade in the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]] continues the live-action incarnation from the {{WPS|Blade (franchise)|''Blade''}} franchise from {{WPS|New Line Cinema}}, who was portrayed by [[Wesley Snipes]] and {{WPS|Sticky Fingaz}}.
**[[Wesley Snipes]] mentioned during an interview with ''{{WPS|Deadline Hollywood}}'' back in July 2015 that he had been in discussions with [[Marvel Studios]] about reprising the role although those films don't share continuity with the [[Marvel Cinematic Universe]].<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20150710071119/http://deadline.com/2015/07/wesley-snipes-blade-comic-con-interview-blade-the-player-1201472733/ Comic Con: Wesley Snipes On ‘Blade’ Marvel Talks, ‘The Player’ & Spike Lee]</ref> However, when [[Mahershala Ali]] was cast as the MCU's version of [[Blade]], Snipes himself approved Ali's casting.<ref>[https://comicbook.com/marvel/2019/07/23/blade-wesley-snipes-comments-on-mahershala-ali-casting/ Exclusive! Wesley Snipes Responds to Marvel Rebooting Blade]</ref>

